560: \(s_*\) by \(-1\); the new ordinary vertical coordinate consequently
561: has the factor \(\epsilon^{nk}\). This is exactly \(A\)'s chain
562: shift and its retained \(A_0\) arrow. The cap unit \(h^{nk}\) acts
563: on a node chart by
564: \((X_j,Y_j)\mapsto(h^{nk}X_j,h^{-nk}Y_j)\); it is an extending
565: unit even though its central value is now \(1-q_\infty\), rather
566: than the constant one of the source-zero calculation. Its origin
567: change has been retained in (IF.30)--(IF.31).
568: 
569: On each remaining ordinary source-base chart, away from the selected
570: disc and these two special pieces, the source and Cartier line are
571: smooth over the full parameter disc. Use their full chain with the
572: actual source/line period. On angular overlaps the affine cochains
573: are exactly those already prescribed by \(F_0\); the only nonzero
574: integral base continuation is the signed cap shift just displayed.
575: The collision overlap has zero such continuation. Consequently
576: the ordinary chains, the collision fan in \(\eta\), and the cap
577: chain glue by literal extending source/line maps. This constructs
578: the partial family over the complement of the selected source-base
579: disc.
580: 
581: Properness over a closed smaller parameter disc follows from the
582: finite compact fundamental covers already described: collision
583: cones and face shells in IF.9, finitely many node/middle chain
584: charts on every ordinary compact source patch, and one finite
585: fundamental chain segment for \(H\) on the cap. A finite source-base
586: cover suffices. Every overlap is the actual biholomorphic map
587: (IF.31), so these compact covers produce a compact inverse image.
588: The only retained real boundary is over the boundary circle of the
589: deleted selected disc.
590: 
591: This is the partial attachment used in the next step. It provides
592: no filling of that last boundary and no return invariant. The exact
593: remaining morphism is its gluing to the selected-cusp scalar trace
594: with the physical Euler line and its normal orientation.
